EXT. SALT DESERT - GENERATION SHIP HULL - DAWN

A ship the size of a mountain lies on its side in a white plain of salt. Its hull, once silver, is rust-red and blistered. Cables hang like dead vines. The sky is a bruised violet. Two moons, one faint, one bright.

At the foot of the hull, a rope ladder dangles. Beyond it, a gaping breach, dark as a throat.

IXCHEL, 30s, wiry, wrapped in a patchwork cloak the color of dust, scales the ladder one handhold at a time. A pack of glass tubes clinks on her back. Her face is scarred with the faint blue lines of old ship-tattoos, a map no one living can read.

At the top, she hauls herself into the breach. Waits for her eyes to adjust.

AMADI, 60s, sits cross-legged in the shadows, so still he seems part of the wall. His skin is dark, his head shaved, his eyes the pale green of old copper. A single lantern glows beside him, powered by something that hums.

IXCHEL
You're real.

AMADI
So are you.

IXCHEL
They said the last one died.

AMADI
They say many things.

She steps closer. The floor, once a corridor, is now a slope of salt-crusted tile.

IXCHEL
I came to see the Bridge.

AMADI
The Bridge is gone.

IXCHEL
The maps say --

AMADI
Maps were written by people who left.

She sets down her pack. Kneels. He studies her tattoos with open interest, like a man reading a letter from a village he forgot.

AMADI (CONT'D)
Whose line are you?

IXCHEL
The Surveyors.

AMADI
Ah. They always came back for a last measurement.

IXCHEL
We came back for the seeds.

AMADI
(softly)
There are no seeds.

IXCHEL
There must be.

AMADI
There was a garden. Then there was a long winter. Then there was me.

He lifts the lantern. The light sweeps a wall, and she sees it: a mural of green, thousands of tiny painted leaves, fading but not gone.

IXCHEL
You painted this.

AMADI
Every day, for forty years.

IXCHEL
Why?

AMADI
So that someone would come and see a garden.

She touches the wall. Her hand leaves a dark print in the dust. Then, slowly, she opens the pack and begins unpacking her glass tubes: soil, water, a single vial of grain.

IXCHEL
Then let me plant what you remember.

Amadi does not answer. He lowers the lantern and begins, very slowly, to weep.
